## A live equation and its plot

The square wave is a sum of odd harmonics. Drag $N$ and watch the partial sum
sharpen toward the square. The equation is rendered offline by KaTeX, the chart by
the vendored Observable Plot, and both react to the same slider:

$$ f(t)=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac{\sin\!\big((2k-1)\,2\pi t\big)}{2k-1}. $$

::: {.panel-tabset}

### Result

{{< input name="harmonics" type="slider" min="1" max="24" step="1" value="5" label="N (harmonics)" >}}

```{js}
//| input: harmonics
//| echo: false
const N = tali.value("harmonics");
const ys = Array.from({ length: 500 }, (_, t) =>
  d3.range(1, N + 1).reduce((s, k) => s + Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * (2 * k - 1) * t / 500) / (2 * k - 1), 0));
return Plot.lineY(ys, { curve: "basis", stroke: "#4c8dff" })
  .plot({ height: 240, marginLeft: 40, x: { label: "t" }, y: { label: "amplitude" } });
```

### Source

```js
//| input: harmonics
//| echo: false
const N = tali.value("harmonics");
const ys = Array.from({ length: 500 }, (_, t) =>
  d3.range(1, N + 1).reduce((s, k) => s + Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * (2 * k - 1) * t / 500) / (2 * k - 1), 0));
return Plot.lineY(ys, { curve: "basis", stroke: "#4c8dff" })
  .plot({ height: 240, marginLeft: 40, x: { label: "t" }, y: { label: "amplitude" } });
```

:::

## Code that explains itself as you read

The reactive chart above is six lines. Scroll this walkthrough and the focus moves
down the code as each line is explained. The scroll-synced highlight is the
framework's `.code-walkthrough` feature, a sticky code panel beside scrolling steps.

::: {.code-walkthrough}

```js
//| input: harmonics
const N = tali.value("harmonics");
const ys = Array.from({ length: 500 }, (_, t) =>
  d3.range(1, N + 1).reduce(
    (s, k) => s + Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * (2 * k - 1) * t / 500) / (2 * k - 1), 0));
return Plot.lineY(ys, { stroke: "#4c8dff" }).plot({ height: 240 });
```

::: {.step lines="1"}
The `//| input: harmonics` directive subscribes this cell to the `harmonics` control. Taliesin re-runs the cell, and only this cell, whenever that value changes.
:::
::: {.step lines="2"}
`tali.value("harmonics")` reads the slider's current number out of the reactive scope.
:::
::: {.step lines="3-5"}
Build the partial sum: for each of 500 sample points, add up the first `N` odd harmonics.
:::
::: {.step lines="6"}
Return an Observable Plot chart. A `{js}` cell mounts whatever DOM node it returns, so there is no glue code between the data and the page.
:::

:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
